WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.947 --> 00:00:11.030
LEE YANCO: Hey, everybody.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.410
Welcome to App 203,
introducing our new managed

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.239
service for Prometheus.

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:17.380
My name is Lee Yanco.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.330
I'm a Product Manager
within Google Cloud.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.240
I'm joined today by Bartosz
Jakubski, Senior DevOps

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.057
Engineer at OpenX.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.140
So today we're going to
talk about some challenges

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.050
with Prometheus, we're going to
introduce Google Cloud Managed

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.302
Service for Prometheus,
do a bit of a deep dive

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:33.760
in the components of the system,
talk about how it compares

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.830
to cloud monitoring,
and then OpenX

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.330
is going to talk
about their journey

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.400
with managed service
for Prometheus.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.310
So, challenges with Prometheus.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.480
Prometheus is great.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:44.620
It works really well.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.280
There is a very good reason
why it is the de facto standard

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.210
for Kubernetes monitoring.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.500
Managing Prometheus for small
deployments is pretty easy.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.770
Managing Prometheus at
scale can be very hard.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.670
So first of all, Prometheus can
be hard to scale horizontally.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.010
When you have a small
cluster, you just

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.183
put a couple of gigs of RAM in
the server and it just works.

00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:07.600
Your cluster gets
a little larger,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.390
you have to scale up by
throwing more RAM at the server.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.520
Eventually your
cluster gets so large

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.560
that you have to shard
by frontend application

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.620
and backend application
or by A through M and N

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.810
through Z or
something like that.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.480
Somewhat interestingly for
a cloud-native service,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.750
Prometheus doesn't have a
particularly good horizontal

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.310
scaling story.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.960
Prometheus can be
hard to make global.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.660
So when you're small and you
only have a few clusters,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:32.510
it's no big deal.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.530
You have a couple of different
data sources within Grafana,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.360
you swap between them, it works.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.150
Eventually you might want to get
a global view, so you federate.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.770
Eventually your Prometheus
deployment grows even more.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.630
So you have multiple
federation servers.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.960
You might simplify it
by switching to Thanos.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.380
So you have Thanos querying
your Prometheus servers.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.300
And then you go
hybrid or multicloud,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.510
and you have Thanos querying
Thanos querying your Prometheus

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:56.530
servers.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.180
And then somebody
on your team adds

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.900
user ID or IP address as a
label, cardinatily explodes,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.640
and the whole thing falls
over, which leads me

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.350
to my third point--
managing Prometheus

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.860
can be an ongoing operational
burden and a thankless task.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.830
Your end customers
do not care how good

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.710
you are at managering/monitoring
infrastructure.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.620
They care how good you are at
keeping your app up, of course.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.570
But the monitoring
infrastructure part

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.010
is really a thankless task.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.670
None of this is hard,
it's just toil, it's work.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.120
It's 20 to 30 hours of
somebody's time, every week,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.390
in perpetuity.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.620
Here's the thing.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.440
Google has been through
this exact experience.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.990
10 years ago, we
also had a system

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.970
for monitoring where we
had local servers that

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:42.500
federated into
regional servers that

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.600
federated into global servers.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.760
We built some new
technology for ourselves

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.470
to solve this
problem for Google.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.960
And we're excited to
share this next-generation

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.750
Prometheus-compatible
solution with you.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.420
So we're introducing Google
Cloud Managed Service

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.670
for Prometheus, a drop-in
replacement for Prometheus

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:00.630
and Thanos.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.495
This is the managed service
for Prometheus ecosystem.

00:03:03.495 --> 00:03:05.370
I'm going to use this
to frame a conversation

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.660
about the different
parts of the system

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.530
and how they benefit
you as a customer.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.380
So in the center sits Monarch.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.310
Monarch is Google's planet-scale
time-series metric storage

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.420
and retrieval database.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.970
Monarch is the same
exact system that we

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.620
use to monitor internal Google.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.190
So ads, search,
networking, YouTube,

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.950
Google Play, you
name it, all use

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.050
Monarch to make sure
that their apps are up

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.960
and running and stable.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.460
We have a separate instance of
monarch called Cloud Monarch.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.290
Cloud Monarch is the
same instance that's

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.550
used by Cloud Monitoring.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.020
And Managed Service
for Prometheus

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.018
shares some of these
cloud monitoring APIs.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.560
And I'll get back
to why that's super

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.060
interesting in the second.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.920
On the left side, we
have the collectors.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.360
So our collector is compatible
anywhere that Prometheus runs.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.850
Because our collector
is Prometheus.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.220
It's Prometheus plus a
couple hundred lines of code

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.810
that wrap things up in a
proto and send it to Google.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.700
So we have two different
types of collectors.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.158
So the self-managed collectors
use your existing Prometheus

00:04:00.158 --> 00:04:03.310
operator configs to
configure the scraping.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.490
So you just copy-paste
those, switch out the binary,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.690
and they just work.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.540
If you're in GKE, we also
have managed collectors,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.840
in which case Google will handle
the scaling, the sharding,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.320
the deployment of
these collectors.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.310
And you configure the
scraping using CRDs.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:21.269
On the query side, because
we have implemented PromQL,

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.940
your existing Grafana
dashboards written in PromQL

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.790
just work, your Prometheus
alerts and alert manager

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.640
configs just work, and
your PromQL-based tools,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.140
any PromQL-based
tool that you might

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.600
find on the internet, any
community-curated dashboard,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.260
any random alert template you
might want to use, just works.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.380
And because we're using the
same APIs and backend systems

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.510
as Cloud Monitoring,
your Cloud Monitoring

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.520
metrics-- your free
metrics, your free BigQuery,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.950
your free Anthos, your free
Istio, your free Dataflow,

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.560
your free Pub/Sub,
whatever you use,

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.870
those metrics will be
accessible within Grafana

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.080
using PromQL, right alongside
your Prometheus metrics,

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.240
giving you a single
pane of glass.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.820
And likewise, because we're
using the same backend system,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.200
your Prometheus
metrics will also

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.790
be available within
Cloud Monitoring.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.090
So you can use advanced features
such as SLO alerting, cloud

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.270
alerting, our Dashboard Builder,
et cetera, with your Prometheus

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.220
metrics in Cloud Monitoring.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.730
We built this to be as drop-in
of a replacement as possible

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.680
so that you can keep
doing what you're doing

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.440
and just outsource the hard
part, the metric storage

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.432
and retrieval part, to us.

00:05:22.432 --> 00:05:24.890
Now let's do a bit of a deep
dive of some of the components

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:25.560
here.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.370
So let's talk about Monarch,
the planet-scale in-memory

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:29.450
time-series database.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.730
It's the same tool we use
to monitor internal Google.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.610
Monarch has over a trillion
time series in RAM,

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.150
14 quadrillion points on
disk, writes 2 and 1/2

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.600
terabytes a second, processes
16 million queries per second.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.930
Whatever you can throw at
us, we can handle, promise.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.750
Monarch is configured for
two-year retention, included

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.760
for all metrics by
default. And Monarch

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.340
has regional storage with
ad hoc global aggregations

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.220
at query time.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.970
So if you say you want your
data to live in cloud zone x,

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.498
your data will live
in cloud zone x.

00:05:59.498 --> 00:06:01.540
On the collection side,
we're compatible anywhere

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:02.650
Prometheus is deployed.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.260
There's two flavors,
managed for GKE environments

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.910
and self-hosted for other
Kubernetes environments.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.570
This means that managed
service for Prometheus

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.600
is hybrid- and
multicloud-compatible

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.467
from the very beginning.

00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:17.050
Our collectors are
open source, so you

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.940
can see what's running within
your clusters of course.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.200
And there's no need to
store any data locally.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.190
So you can use way less
RAM to run these collectors

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.120
than normal Prometheus.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.310
On the query side, we're
fully PromQL compatible.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.040
So your existing Grafana
dashboards just work.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.743
You can query your
free GCP metrics

00:06:33.743 --> 00:06:35.160
alongside your
Prometheus metrics,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.618
getting you a single
pane of glass.

00:06:36.618 --> 00:06:38.430
And read permissions
are enforced on groups

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.180
of projects at query time.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.180
So this is really
nice for customers

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.340
that have tenants of their own.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.490
So you can do something
like send tenant 1's metrics

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.910
to project A, send tenant
2's metrics to project B.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.400
And then you have a
separate permissions

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.420
that has both project A and
B included for your SRE teams

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.313
to get a global view
across all your tenants.

00:07:01.313 --> 00:07:02.980
On the alerting and
recording rule side,

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.470
we work with Prometheus
Alert Manager out of the box.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.770
We work with PromQL-based
alerts and recording rules,

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.520
anything you might
have developed already,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.630
out of the box.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.580
And we are compatible
with Google Cloud's

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.670
alerting as well.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.270
So as you can see here, we
built an interactive query UI

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.470
within Cloud Console.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.180
You can see this is
working software.

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.370
This is me querying
Prometheus metrics.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:25.320
And you can see them
returning to the screen.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.470
We also have full
Grafana compatibility.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:31.220
So you can see this is
me just changing the data

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.320
source from local to foreign.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.160
And it just works.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.880
Nothing changes.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.570
So this is great for you as
a customer, terrible for me

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:39.290
as a presenter.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:40.832
Because really
nothing should change.

00:07:40.832 --> 00:07:42.498
When you swap from
your local Prometheus

00:07:42.498 --> 00:07:44.810
to Managed Service for
Prometheus as your data source,

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:46.640
it should be exactly
the same data.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.482
And that's what you're
seeing right here.

00:07:49.482 --> 00:07:51.690
So let's do a brief comparison
with Cloud Monitoring,

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:56.070
because we do now have two
products within the same area.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.460
So there are two products
coming out around the same time.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:01.980
There's Google Cloud Managed
Service for Prometheus, which

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.620
is Google's recommended way
to run Prometheus at scale,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.680
and there's Cloud Monitoring
and the upcoming GKE Workload

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.720
Metrics, which is
Google's recommended way

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.010
to monitor Kubernetes
applications.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.400
So maybe another way to
think of this might be,

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.870
if you, as a customer, if
you're saying to yourself,

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.350
you know what, I don't want
to deal with any monitoring

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.180
infrastructure
whatsoever, I want

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.220
Google to manage
everything for me

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:26.790
and I want to get the best
practices of Google monitoring

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:29.490
and Google SRE's philosophy
built into my monitoring

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.309
infrastructure, then Cloud
Monitoring and GKE Workload

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:33.845
Metrics is the product for you.

00:08:33.845 --> 00:08:35.220
If you're saying
to yourself, you

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.250
know what, I like Prometheus, I
like the Prometheus ecosystem,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.840
I want to keep what
I have already,

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.510
I just want to outsource the
hard part of metric storage

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:45.420
and retrieval scaling, then
Managed Service for Prometheus

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.650
is the product for you.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:50.460
Another way to think of this
might be, how much do you

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:51.715
want to be managed?

00:08:51.715 --> 00:08:53.340
So with Managed
Service for Prometheus,

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.410
you get scalable
collections, global queries,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.560
and long-term retention included
as a managed service, of course

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.450
with open-source interfaces.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.090
Whereas with Cloud Monitoring
and GKE Workload Metrics,

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.400
you also get alert
management, dashboarding,

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.190
and unified metrics, logs,
and traces as managed services

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:09.393
as well.

00:09:09.393 --> 00:09:11.310
Remember, because we're
using the same backend

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.100
and share a lot of the same
APIs, swapping between these

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:14.970
is pretty easy.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.040
So you're never locked
into your decision.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:22.080
You can choose the best solution
for you at any given time.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.400
We are in preview right now.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.500
We expect to be in
general availability

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.130
sometime early next year.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.890
But we would love to
have you in preview.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:33.060
You can sign up at
g.co/cloud/managedprometheus.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:37.075
Once again, that's
g.co/cloud/managedprometheus.

00:09:37.075 --> 00:09:38.700
So I'm going to turn
it over to Bartosz

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.770
now to talk about
OpenX's journey

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:44.637
with Managed Service for
Prometheus in preview.

00:09:44.637 --> 00:09:46.720
BARTOSZ JAKUBSKI: Hi, my
name is Bartosz Jakubski,

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.075
and I'm a senior DevOps
Engineer at OpenX.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:55.300
OpenX is a global
advertising exchange.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:59.890
We handle over 200 billion
dynamic requests per day.

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.905
In 2019, we completed our
move from on-premises servers

00:10:03.905 --> 00:10:04.405
to GCP.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.240
Before moving to cloud,
we've had a custom monitoring

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.220
solution which is
based on Graphite,

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.890
and use Grafana now
for visualization.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.680
We've used migration to
GCP as an opportunity

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:21.850
to update our monitoring stack
to Prometheus-based solution.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:22.970
We're a global company.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:26.560
So our monitoring
spans many GKE clusters

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.980
in multiple GCP regions
on three continents.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:35.950
And right now we ingest over
350,000 samples per second.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:37.960
We do use Cloud
Monitoring too, mainly

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:40.765
for metrics from
services managed by GCP.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:46.000
When it comes to
Prometheus, we run

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.600
what I'd call a pretty standard
global Prometheus setup.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:52.060
We use Thanos for
long-term storage

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.790
of metrics and global view.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:59.350
We do use Grafana with about
100 dashboards right now.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.470
And majority of our
other team works on data

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.060
from more than one GKE cluster.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:08.490
So it's usually quite global.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.440
And we store about two
years of metrics right now.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.780
We really do like our stack.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.600
Grafana, Prometheus, and
Thanos work well together

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:19.650
in the cloud.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.440
Most importantly, it just works.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.520
But there are some issues.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.550
Running full kind of stack can
be quite an involved process.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:33.360
You can see here a list
of Thanos components

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.780
that are running in our
global observability cluster.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:40.680
You have to deploy, manage,
and monitor all of them.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:42.796
In some cases, like
with Thanos start,

00:11:42.796 --> 00:11:45.240
you have to manually
shard instances by time

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.530
to ensure they won't
run out of memory

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:51.390
and performance
will be adequate.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.610
Scaling, managing
resource requirements,

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:55.470
modifying sharding
configuration,

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.560
is something we have
to do regularly.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.810
Just keeping the lights
on does require work.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:04.260
For example,
Kubernetes upgrades may

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:08.220
require modifications and
manifests for our components.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.490
There's always
some effort needed.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.810
We do have some
performance issues.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.540
Some of them could be solved
on modifying our setup.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:23.510
But again, it needs time
and resources to do so.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.580
Due to a large amount
of components involved,

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.620
our global alerting
and monitoring

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:31.850
is a bit more fragile
than we'd like.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:35.660
Also, for global setup,
intercluster connectivity

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.220
is required.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:39.290
It is another piece of
cloud infrastructure

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.095
that you have to set
up and secure yourself.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.510
Also, more components
and more involved setup

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:50.210
means that more
knowledge or more time

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.240
is required to make
changes or fix issues.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.960
Why we decided to use Managed
Service for Prometheus

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:02.970
in preview.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.950
First, as a company,
we are always

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.080
interested in reducing
complexity and maintenance

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.310
burden.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:11.700
One way to achieve that is
by using managed services

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:16.710
instead of rolling out and
maintaining our own ones.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:20.970
Another reason is Prometheus
query language compatibility.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.370
This is a big
selling point for us.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.980
Complete migration to
any other query language

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.790
would require a major effort.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.630
And ability to run
unmodified queries

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:31.875
makes the move possible.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.350
Self-managed collector
is just Prometheus.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.620
You just deploy it like you
deploy your regular Prometheus.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.140
It can run alongside
your Prometheus.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.170
And to do so, it only
requires small changes

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.920
in existing configuration.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.280
So we installed collectors
in our production clusters,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:57.950
gathered some data, added
a data source to Grafana,

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:01.930
and we got the same
graph basically.

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:09.420
So it turns out that Grafana
dashboards indeed do work.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:14.160
We do use more advanced
PromQL queries in some places.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.800
And their compatibility
is pretty good too.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:24.855
So what opportunities for OpenX
Managed Service for Prometheus

00:14:24.855 --> 00:14:25.355
provides.

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.650
This is a simplified
diagram of components

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:34.710
that we are running in our
global monitoring stack.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.420
If we were to move to Managed
Service for Prometheus,

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.310
in transitioning period, when
we will still need access

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:44.670
to our older data in
cloud storage bucket,

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:48.060
some components can
already be eliminated.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.690
Once we are fully migrated to
Managed Service for Prometheus,

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.870
most of the components will
disappear with only Prometheus

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:55.980
collectors remaining.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:03.550
This reduction in complexity
will get us some time back.

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.910
We will no longer need to adjust
storage volumes and resource

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:07.870
requests.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:10.570
There will be no need to
modify sharding in Thanos start

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.210
instances.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.790
Fewer components
means fewer incidents

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:19.480
caused by problems in alerting
and monitoring system.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.820
Last but not least,
better integration

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:24.520
with Cloud Monitoring
would allow us not only

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:28.570
utilize Cloud Monitoring
tools like SLO alerting,

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.630
but would also simplify
and streamline access

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.760
to metric data.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.750
No more combining two query
languages in single graph

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:41.920
in Grafana to combine our
application metrics with,

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:45.890
for example, load
balancer metrics.

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.740
Thank you for letting me
share my OpenX Prometheus

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.240
story with you.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.020
Now back to Lee.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.270
LEE YANCO: Thank you, Bartosz.

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.580
Once again, we would love
to have you for the preview.

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:01.170
You can sign up at
g.co/cloud/managedprometheus.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.230
Thank you for watching.

